 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0200

phi-adapter-sdk (0.21.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * AdapterFlag is split in two, because it was two things with one owner
    each. AdapterCapability is what a plugin declares about itself -
    compiled in, identical for every instance, re-announced on every start:
    CloudServices, RequiresPolling, SupportsDiscovery, SupportsProbe and the
    four rename capabilities. AdapterOption is what a household answers for
    one instance and no plugin ever states: UseTls and EnableLogs. The bit
    values are unchanged in both, gaps and all, because the numbers are on
    the wire and moving them would buy nothing but tidiness.
  * They are kept apart by type now rather than by a mask someone has to
    remember. An instance used to hold a copy of both; nothing refreshed the
    capability half, and the first build that grew a capability refused
    every room, group and scene rename on boxes whose adapters predated it.
    A capability can no longer be assigned to an instance's field at all.
  * v1::Adapter::flags is therefore v1::Adapter::options, and the adapter
    object core sends down carries `options` where it carried `flags`.
    AdapterCapabilities::flags keeps its name - `caps.flags` reads
    unambiguously - and changes type.

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 22:15:00 +0200

phi-adapter-sdk (0.20.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A name may only be changed where the change lands on the thing itself,
    so that phi, the maker's app and any other hub read the same one. Until
    now only devices could carry a rename downward (CmdDeviceNameUpdate);
    rooms, groups and scenes had no way at all, so a name changed in phi
    would have been silently overwritten by the next sync. CmdEntityNameUpdate
    (0x0208) carries one for all three - the payload is the same for each, so
    which of them it is rides in `kind` (NamedEntityKind) - and
    AdapterInstance::onEntityNameUpdate is the hook that answers it. The base
    class answers "not implemented", as it does for every other command.
  * AdapterFlag::SupportsRename is gone, replaced by SupportsDeviceRename,
    SupportsRoomRename, SupportsGroupRename and SupportsSceneRename: one bit
    could not say that an adapter renames its devices but not its rooms, and
    core now refuses a rename it knows cannot land instead of storing a name
    only phi would know. An adapter that is an exception for one entity says
    so on the entity, with `fixedName` in its meta, the way a device behind a
    bridge already did.

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 21:05:00 +0200

phi-adapter-sdk (0.19.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The adapter log switch gates the chatter, not the vocabulary: Info, Warn
    and Error now reach core whatever AdapterFlag::EnableLogs says, while
    Trace and Debug wait for it and for logging.minLevel/categories. It used
    to gate everything below Error, which left an adapter with the switch off
    nothing to say - and five adapters answered that by writing to stderr,
    where core stamps every line Warn and no filter applies.
  * PROTOCOLL.md: adapter code must not use stderr for adapter-domain logging.

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 12:40:00 +0200

phi-adapter-sdk (0.18.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * sdk::Reachability (phi/adapter/sdk/reachability.h): what an adapter does
    when a device stops answering, in one place. Counts the misses in a row,
    hands back the wait before the next attempt, and says when the state
    turned and when a line is worth writing. No clock and no timer of its
    own, in the manner of sdk::ButtonPresses: the adapter passes the time it
    has and arms the timer it owns, and keeps the decision what counts as an
    answer. One per thing that can go away, not one per adapter.

