 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 19:00:43 +0200

phi-adapter-sdk (0.13.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * HttpClient (the loop client) speaks TLS, through the same net_tls
    decisions as fetch(): chain always checked, a named CA in addition to
    the system store, hostname check off is not accept anything. A
    Call::tlsServerName names the certificate to expect when it is not the
    URL host - a Hue bridge dialled by IP certifies its bridge id.
  * HttpClient::stream(): the head, then body pieces as they arrive,
    decoded, for a server-sent event stream; done when it ends.
  * A host with several addresses is tried address by address; an IPv6
    address nobody listens on is not a dead peer.
  * sdk::ButtonPresses: the button-event policy as one shared machine,
    500 ms either/or, no clock of its own.
  * No ABI change for existing symbols; HttpClient::Call grew fields, so
    adapters using it are rebuilt against this version.

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 18:12:49 +0200

phi-adapter-sdk (0.12.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * net::fetch(): the whole HTTP exchange on the calling thread, with TLS and
    redirects. phi-core had written all of this a second time - a request
    line, both body framings, the redirect rules and the certificate checks -
    and two things that read bytes off a network eventually disagree about one
    of them.
  * It shares the framing with HttpClient rather than repeating it:
    serializeRequest() and ResponseParser, so a chunked body means the same on
    the loop path and the blocking one. What still differs between the two is
    only how a caller waits.
  * TLS settings come from v1::TlsSettings instead of a bare CA path, and the
    verification decisions moved into one place (src/net_tls.h) - among them
    the one worth not making twice: turning off the hostname check leaves the
    chain check on. A named certificate is now trusted in addition to the
    system store rather than instead of it, which is what the contract's own
    description of the field already said.
  * OpenSSL::SSL is linked, not only OpenSSL::Crypto. No ABI change: symbols
    were added, the soname is unchanged, and adapters built against 0.11.0
    keep running.

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 14:44:11 +0200

phi-adapter-sdk (0.11.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * ChannelDataType::Json and sendChannelObjectStateUpdated(): a composite
    channel value, for the kinds whose meaning does not fit in one number. A
    firmware update is a status and two versions, and reporting only one of
    them loses the other two.
  * The value is a flat set of named scalars, serialized by the SDK. Not a
    general escape hatch: the shape cannot break the envelope it travels in,
    and a projection field has to name a scalar anyway.
  * channelProjectionField(kind) names the one field that carries a composite
    value's scalar meaning - `status` for DeviceSoftwareUpdate. Everything that
    reduces a value to one comparable thing (a history row, an automation
    condition) reads it from there, so the adapter, the rules and the history
    cannot disagree about what the channel is.
  * Colour keeps its own type and its own transport; it predates this and has
    no scalar meaning to project.

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Mon, 08 Sep 2026 12:45:00 +0200

